import json
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]; out=root/'outputs'; results=json.loads((out/'experiments/controlled/metrics.json').read_text())
lines=['# Causal Pinball: controlled JEPA experiment','', '## Hypothesis', '', 'Intervention labels may improve one-step latent prediction, and visually learned object slots may encode physical state better than a global video embedding. These are predictive hypotheses, not tests of causal identification.', '', '## Architecture and data', '', 'The dataset has 2,400 training, 300 validation and 300 test independently seeded sequences, each 16 RGB frames at 64×64. This experiment uses the first four frames and three adjacent transitions per sequence. A is a global CNN JEPA. B replaces global pooling with eight learned attention slots and a relational transformer. C adds an embedding of the five intervention indicators for the corresponding transition. B+I is the exact same implementation and objective as C: there is no intervention-specific loss and no valid separate causal-objective ablation. Slots are not guaranteed to correspond to physical objects or stable identities. Simulator states are used only for frozen probes.', '', '## Training methodology', '', 'All runs used seed 42, batch size 8, 5 epochs (1,500 optimizer steps), AdamW at 0.0003, the same training split and MPS. Checkpoints were selected by validation MSE plus variance and covariance penalties; no test data were used for selection. The target encoder is stop-gradient and updated with EMA 0.99. A variance floor and covariance penalty discourage, but cannot guarantee avoidance of, collapse. Earlier pilots had near-zero standard deviation or covariance rank near 1. Larger pilots achieved nonzero variance but remained low-rank. Variance is measured across frames and sequences, pooling slots.', '', 'The frozen ridge probe is fit on representations and ball x/y position and velocity labels from the training split only, with fixed regularization, and evaluated on the test split. R² may be negative. Persistence uses the target encoder representation of the current frame to predict the next. Interventions are binary indicators for t→t+1; no controlled counterfactual pairs exist here. Confidence intervals bootstrap sequences, not individual frames.', '', '## Results: test split', '', '| Model | Epoch | JEPA MSE [95% CI] | Persistence MSE | Intervention MSE | No intervention MSE | Std | Effective rank / 32 | Probe R²: x, y, vx, vy |', '|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|']
for k,r in results.items():
 ci=r['prediction_mse_bootstrap_95ci']; p=', '.join(f'{v:.3f}' for v in r['probe']['r2']); lines.append(f"| {k} | {r['checkpoint_epoch']} | {r['prediction_mse']:.5f} [{ci[0]:.5f}, {ci[1]:.5f}] | {r['persistence_mse']:.5f} | {r['intervention_mse']:.5f} | {r['nonintervention_mse']:.5f} | {r['mean_embedding_std']:.4f} | {r['effective_covariance_rank']:.2f} | {p} |")
lines += ['', 'MSE values have model-specific latent scales and should not be ranked directly across models. Compare each prediction MSE to its own persistence baseline. Intervened and non-intervened transitions are observational subsets, not matched treatment effects. B+I and C use the same model and seed, so their equality is expected, not evidence of an effect.', '', '## Failure cases and limitations', '', 'A single seed and short run cannot establish statistical robustness. Low effective rank indicates substantial dimensional concentration even where per-dimension variance exceeds the heuristic threshold. Learned attention slots may collapse onto similar content; no object-discovery or identity metric was established. Moving target embeddings change MSE scales through training, making loss-curve comparisons difficult. No autoregressive latent transition or visual decoder is implemented, so no multi-step rollout or generated future frame is claimed. Training steps are matched but architecture-dependent compute is not identical. Device allocation logs cover MPS driver allocation during batches, not whole-system unified memory.', '', '## Suggested Substack narrative', '', 'Lead with the finding that an apparently excellent predictive loss can hide collapsed representations. Show how variance and effective rank change that interpretation, then compare each model against persistence and inspect frozen physical-state probes. Explain that intervention conditioning supplies information, while a causal claim would require a separately validated objective and suitable intervention or counterfactual evaluation.', '', '## Reproduce', '', 'From the repository root, see `scripts/controlled_experiment.py`, `scripts/audit_experiment.py`, and `scripts/evaluate_controlled.py`. Results and exact run configurations are in `outputs/experiments/`; figures are in `outputs/figures/`. To reproduce the four full-split runs with fresh output names:', '', '```sh', 'for m in a b b_info c; do .venv/bin/python scripts/controlled_experiment.py --model "$m" --name "replicate_$m" --epochs 5 --patience 10; done', '.venv/bin/python scripts/evaluate_controlled.py --names replicate_a replicate_b replicate_b_info replicate_c --group replicate --split test', '```']
(out/'research_summary.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
